  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonable conditions continue through tonight.\n\nPersistent WNW flow continues through this evening, bringing \nseasonable temperatures and dry conditions ahead of the next storm. \nHigh temperatures today remain similar to yesterday... in the mid \n50s. A dry shortwave pushing through before sunrise will allow for \nsome wind gusts 20 to 25 mph in the higher elevations and coastal \nplain through mid-morning before conditions slowly calm through the \nafternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A storm system will bring a prolonged period of rain \nto much of southern New England Thursday through Saturday with \nperiods of snow in the higher elevations. Significant accumulation \nis not expected. \n\nA vertically stacked trough of low pressure over the Ohio Valley \nwill pass over the Mid-Atlantic on Thursday, strengthening as is \nmoves into the waters south of New England Thursday night and lifts \ninto the Gulf of Maine Friday night. This leads to a prolonged \nperiod of unsettled weather, even continuing into Saturday thanks to \nthe slow progression/trailing behind of the upper trough/cold pool \naloft. Light to moderate warm advection rain will expand over \nsouthern New England on Thursday as the low approaches. Eventually a \ncooler airmass is pulled down as flow around the low backs to the \nnorth and northwest while the mid/upper levels cool with the arrival \nof the trough. This, together with some diabatic cooling will allow \nfor places in the high terrain of the Berkshires and Worcester Hills \nto flip to snow by Thursday night. However, given the lack of \nantecedent cold air in the boundary layer those outside of the \nhigher elevations will more likely experience a cold rain for most \nif not all of the period. The decidedly mild airmass in place over \nthe last week will not bode well for much in the way of \naccumulation. Model soundings show a lack of omega in the snow \ngrowth zone as well as a dry slot which may move in overnight \nThursday cutting into potential snowfall amounts. Ensemble guidance \nis fairly steady in showing only a few outlier members with \nsubstantial snowfall across MA/CT/RI; probabilities of AOA 3\" in the \nWorcester Hills range from 10-30% (both EPS and GEFS) but keeping in \nmind these are using 10:1 SLR while in reality they would be less. \nIn early season situations like this it is often useful to utilize \nthe positive snow depth change guidance as a more realistic \nexpectation; thus, continuing to expect a low impact event with only \nminor accumulations in the highest terrain. That being said, as we \nget in range of the higher resolution guidance we will continue to \nevaluate and update the forecast based on the newest\ninformation. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...High pressure brings cool and dry conditions back to \nsouthern New England for the start of the next work week.\n\nAs the low slowly pushes further into Atlantic Canada, high pressure \nbegins to build over the Great Lakes Sunday into Monday. The tighter \npressure gradient will allow blustery conditions to linger through \nSunday, but the strengthening high will mark the return of a dry \nperiod through the start of the next work week. High temps slowly \nclimb through the early week... in the upper 40s Sunday to the mid \n50s Tuesday/Wednesday.",
